"[{\"id\":\"3933110402\",\"title\":\"Can I delete or unlink a fixture from a scheduled voyage without deleting the voyage?\",\"text\":\"No. Once a cargo fixture is linked to a voyage, it creates a permanent connection between the cargo and the voyage. This means the fixture cannot be unlinked or deleted independently of the voyage. Additionally, the Opr Type of a voyage can only be changed from TCOV to TCTO if there is no fixture number associated with it. In all other cases, changing the Opr Type or removing the fixture requires deleting both the voyage and the fixture.\"},{\"id\":\"2276622415\",\"title\":\"Carbon Calculator 'Include in PnL' by default\",\"text\":\"We have introduced CFGDefaultIncludeCarbonExpInPnl to default Include In PnL on the Carbon Calculator. Below is no longer a necessary workflow. Some clients may want the &lsquo;Include in PnL' in the Carbon Calculator to be selected by default. To do so, clients would need to create a .TDEFAULT estimate that has the box checked. Please refer to the following steps: In an Estimate column view, enter .TDEFAULT as the Estimate ID. Click Save. If there is an existing .TDEFAULT , search for the estimates using the Find Estimates panel and then click its Estimate ID link or row. [image] Enter the details view. In order for the &lsquo;Include in PnL' to appear in the carbon calculator, the system will require a vessel to be selected. Select a random Vessel (Can create a dummy vessel that does not have any data) and check the &lsquo;Include in PnL' checkbox and click &lsquo;Apply'. Remove the vessel and click &lsquo;Save'. [image] To confirm that the &lsquo;Include in PnL' checkbox has been saved as default. Open a new tab and create a new estimate with another vessel. Go to Menu >Analytics> Carbon Calculator [image]\"},{\"id\":\"4816470054\",\"title\":\"Discrepancy Between Net Voyage Days in Fixture and TCO Estimate Explained\",\"text\":\"When a TC-Out (TCO) estimate is fixed and linked to a voyage, users may notice that the net voyage days in the fixture/voyage are different from the days shown in the estimate . This article explains why this discrepancy occurs and how to resolve it. Why do net voyage days in the fixture differ from the estimate? The discrepancy is caused by a combination of two factors: The TCO duration in the estimate has been manually amended (e.g. a trader extended the duration from the original itinerary-based calculation to reflect a longer contracted period). The estimate is then fixed as a Trip TC contract rather than a Period TC contract. When the TCO duration is manually amended in the estimate and the fixture is created as a Trip TC , IMOS calculates net voyage days in the voyage based on the original itinerary alone, ignoring the manually adjusted duration. This results in fewer days being reflected in the fixture. Example Original itinerary-based TCO duration: 40.77 days Trader manually amends TCO duration in estimate to: 75 days Estimate fixed as Trip TC &rarr; Net voyage days in fixture: 40.77 days Estimate fixed as Period TC &rarr; Net voyage days in fixture: 75 days [image][image] What happens if the TCO duration is NOT manually amended? If the TCO duration in the estimate is not manually amended, the net voyage days will transfer correctly to the fixture regardless of whether the estimate is fixed as a Trip TC or Period TC contract. No discrepancy will occur in this scenario. [image][image] How do I fix the discrepancy? Option A &mdash; Fix the estimate as a Period TC contract If the trader has manually amended the TCO duration in the estimate, fix the estimate as Period TC instead of Trip TC. This ensures the full amended TCO duration carries over to the voyage. Open the estimate in IMOS. Locate the contract type field in the fixture properties. Select Period TC as the contract type. Fix the estimate. The net voyage days in the voyage will now reflect the manually amended TCO duration. Option B &mdash; Amend the voyage itinerary to add idle days If you prefer to keep the contract type as Trip TC , you can instead amend the voyage itinerary directly to include idle/waiting days so that the total itinerary duration matches the intended TCO duration. Open the voyage in IMOS. Navigate to the voyage itinerary/schedule. Add idle or waiting port days to account for the additional duration. Save &mdash; the net voyage days will update to reflect the adjusted itinerary.\"},{\"id\":\"1547895032\",\"title\":\"Error: Voyage could not be loaded after deleting the TC contract the voyage was created from.\",\"text\":\"If you are receiving an error, \\\"Voyage could not be loaded.\\\" when trying to open an existing voyage, it could be a result of deleting and updating the time charter contract that the voyage was created from. There are checks in place to prevent deletion of a time charter contract once it has been used in a voyage, but simultaneously updating the voyage in question can result in the deletion of the TC contract from the voyage. The error appears because a key piece of contractual information is missing from the voyage. If you want the voyage linked to a different time charter contract than the contract the voyage was created from, the time charter contract should be removed from the voyage before deleting the time charter contract from the system. Example Scenario: Voyage is created using TC contract \\\"A,\\\" but a user would like to update the TC contract to contract \\\"B\\\" A user deletes TC contract \\\"A\\\" (This may occur if a user has been creating the TCO voyage while another was simultaneously deleting the TCO fixture.) When trying to open the Voyage to link TC contract \\\"B,\\\" you receive the error message: Voyage could not be loaded. (The voyage cannot be loaded because a key piece of data is missing) To avoid the error message, create a new TC for the voyage, which will automatically assign TC Code \\\"A\\\", and after saving the fixture, the voyage can be opened. If the user does not want the voyage linked to the TC, they should remove the TC from the voyage before deleting the TC fixture.\"},{\"id\":\"906723396\",\"title\":\"How are estimated Port days calculated?\",\"text\":\"The estimated Port days field in the Estimate is calculated using the following formula: EstPD = [(L/D Qty/ L/D Rate) + TT] x Terms Where. Estimated Port Days = EstPD Loaded or Dischared Cargo quantity = L/D Qty Loading/Discharging rate = LD disch rate Turn Time = TT Laytime Terms = SHEX, SHINC etc. The Laytime Terms are defined in the Data Center by Veson and they are measured by a Factor. [image] Example: The below screenshot displayed the port itinerary in a Voyage Estimate. [image] EstPD = [(L/D Qty/ L/D Rate) + TT] x Terms, which translates in: 5.09 days = [(80,000 mts/ 20,000 mts per day) + 0] x 1.2727 Depending on the cargo terms of the business scenario, you may need to alter the calculation formula by enabling the Configuration Flag , CFGVoyapiNoShexFactorOnTurntime. Doing so will alter the way the estimated port days is calculated: EstPD = ((Cgo Qty / LD rate) x Laytime Term) + TT\"},{\"id\":\"4040753245\",\"title\":\"How are Miscellaneous Expenses calculated in Deviation Estimates P&L?\",\"text\":\"In the Deviation Estimates P&L, the Miscellaneous Expenses line includes the sum of two components: Cargo Expenses Miscellaneous Expenses [image] These two categories are combined because, in the current setup, there is no dedicated line item for Cargo Expenses in the Estimate's P&L. As a result, the system merges them under the Miscellaneous Expenses label for reporting purposes.\"},{\"id\":\"675709409\",\"title\":\"How are pricing terms selected?\",\"text\":\"Home > Chartering FAQs > How are pricing terms selected? On the Pricing tab of a Cargo , VC In , or COA , the pricing row is selected based on a scoring system: If a pricing row perfectly matches an itinerary (Load Port(s), Discharge Port(s), and Cargoes match), the first such row is used and all others are ignored. The scoring, in this case, is not relevant. If a perfect match is not found, the scoring is as follows: Load Port(s)/Discharge Port(s) score is calculated as: If there is more than one itinerary L or D port found in the pricing row, +1 If the pricing row has no L or D ports specified, +1 If there is a single itinerary L or D port, and it matches the first pricing row L or D port, +2 Otherwise (i.e., ports are specified and do not match), -10 (i.e., disqualified) Cargo score is calculated as: If the cargo is specified and matches, +2 If the cargo is not specified, +1 Otherwise (i.e., a cargo is specified and does not match), -10 (i.e., disqualified)\"},{\"id\":\"3838148914\",\"title\":\"How can I adjust the commence date of a consecutive Voyage?\",\"text\":\"When the consecutive voyage checkbox is selected, the current voyage is linked to the previous. [image] This means the initial bunkers, start date/time and some other values are taken from the previous voyage. To amend the start time of a consecutive voyage, you will need to adjust the end time of the previous linked voyage.\"},{\"id\":\"3838083345\",\"title\":\"How can I deselect the Intercompany checkbox on a TC Contract?\",\"text\":\"The Intercompany checkbox is used to link two TC contracts together. [image] This checkbox can only be unchecked if there are no invoices or voyages linked to the respective TCs, otherwise you will receive this error. [image]\"},{\"id\":\"1792934274\",\"title\":\"How does Advanced Pricing rules in VC In COA and Cargo contract impact demurrage/despatch rates?\",\"text\":\"There are several options within the VC In COA and Cargo contract that determine the demurrage/despatch rate for a Cargo contract. These options are: VC In COA - Advanced VC In COA - Advanced Pricing Overrides &lsquo;Demurrage/Despatch' Cargo Contract - Use Pricing from COA Cargo Contract - Advanced [image][image] Below is a matrix for the permutations/combinations of the checkboxes: S/N VC In COA checkboxes Cargo Contract checkboxes Cargo Contract takes demurrage /despatch rates from: Able to manually edit demurrage /despatch rates in Cargo Contract? (1) Advanced (2) Advanced Pricing Overrides &lsquo;Demurrage/Despatch' (3) Use Pricing from COA (4) Advanced 1 Checked Checked Checked Checked VC In COA No 2 Checked Checked Not checked Not checked Cargo contract; user must key in manually Yes 3 Checked Checked Not checked Checked Cargo contract; user must key in manually Yes 4 Checked Checked Checked Not checked Scenario does not exist, as checked &lsquo;Use Pricing from COA' will auto-check &lsquo;Advanced' - 5 Checked Not checked Checked Checked Cargo contract (populated from Advanced Pricing rule) No 6 Checked Not checked Not checked Checked Cargo contract (populated from Advanced Pricing rule) Yes 7 Checked Not checked Not checked Not checked Cargo contract; user must key in manually Yes 8 Checked Not checked Checked Not checked Scenario does not exist, as checked &lsquo;Use Pricing from COA' will auto-check &lsquo;Advanced' - From the above matrix, we can deduce that: If Use Pricing from COA checkbox is checked in the cargo contract, the operator will not able to edit the demurrage/despatch rates manually in the cargo contract. The VC In COA's demurrage/despatch rates will override the rates in each cargo contract if the checkboxes (2) Advanced Pricing Overrides &lsquo;Demurrage/Despatch' and (3) Use Pricing from COA are checked. You may refer to VIP - Advanced Pricing for more information on the Advanced Pricing Overrides function.\"},{\"id\":\"4057301046\",\"title\":\"How does the system calculate LS Sea Day greater than Total Sea Days in Estimate?\",\"text\":\"If the LS Sea Days value is greater than the Total Sea Days, please check the weather factor specified in the estimate. The Total Sea Days reflect the actual number of days spent at sea during the voyage, while the LS Sea Days are adjusted based on the weather factor included in the estimate. This factor accounts for potential delays or slower speeds due to adverse weather conditions. [image]\"},{\"id\":\"654213373\",\"title\":\"How do I manually change the Bill By field in the Cargo of a mirrored relet voyage?\",\"text\":\"Home > Chartering FAQs > How do I manually change the Bill By field in the Cargo of a mirrored relet voyage? In a mirrored relet voyage , updates to Cargo contracts across both the operating and relet voyages are usually driven by the Cargo contract on the operating voyage. If you are trying to change the Bill By field for the Cargo contract in a mirrored relet voyage but the field is locked, you can do so manually by clicking the value of the CP Qty/Unit field. The CP Quantity Details form appears, on which you can manually change the Bill By field: [image]\"},{\"id\":\"637337849\",\"title\":\"How is Daily Cost determined on the Speed Comparison Analysis form?\",\"text\":\"Home > Chartering FAQs > How is Daily Cost determined on the Speed Comparison Analysis form? On the Speed Comparison Analysis form, Daily Cost is an average of the Hire Rate found on the Time Charter (TC) contract linked to the Estimate. Example The TC In Hire Rate is $4,000 for the first 10 days and $10,000 for the next 10 days: [image] If the Estimate commences exactly at the start of the hire period (04/01/17 12:00), then the average Daily Cost is calculated to be $7,000 for the 20 voyage days: [($4,000 * 10 Days) + ($10,000 * 10 Days)] / 20 Total Days = $7,000/Day [image]\"},{\"id\":\"3933732867\",\"title\":\"How Should I Handle a Change in TC Owner When a TC Invoice has Already Been Raised?\",\"text\":\"Once a Time Charter invoice has been raised, the owner field in the contract will be locked and it will no longer be possible to change the TC owner. Additionally, a single voyage cannot be linked to more than one Time Charter contract. Background: why this happens When a vessel's legal owning entity changes, the counterparty on the charter changes too. This is a formal, documented process &mdash; the outgoing and incoming owners typically go through a legal/audit review, and actual redelivery/delivery certificates are issued and endorsed by the vessel's master at the \\\"change of hands\\\" on board. Because the counterparty is different, the cleanest approach &mdash; and the one this article reflects &mdash; is to treat it as a redelivery from the old owner followed by a new delivery from the new owner, rather than trying to edit the existing TC In contract mid-stream. To handle a change in TC ownership in this scenario, follow these steps: Update the existing TCI contract by modifying the redelivery port to reflect the port where the vessel was located at the time the ownership changed. Update the voyage itinerary by setting this port as the final port of the voyage and assigning it port function Z (Redelivery) . Remove all other ports from the voyage itinerary to reflect the redelivery. Create a new TCI contract under the new owner's name, starting from the redelivery port of the previous contract. The new delivery date/time should match the redelivery date/time on the old contract, consistent with the actual on-board redelivery/delivery certificates. Schedule a new voyage and link it to the new TCI contract . This voyage should start from the same port, using port function Y (Delivery) . Important limitation: Statement of Account (SoA) Because the old and new owner are on two separate TC In contracts, you cannot print a single SoA that spans from the original vessel delivery (under the old owner) through to the redelivery under the new owner's contract. The SoA is scoped to each contract individually, so it will only ever cover one owner's period at a time. This should be flagged to the client up front so they aren't expecting a single consolidated statement across the ownership change. Recommendation Settle all outstanding hire/invoices with the old owner under the original TCI contract up to the redelivery date. Going forward, run settlements with the new owner under the new TCI contract from the delivery date. This keeps each owner's books clean and avoids trying to reconcile a single SoA across two different counterparties. By following these steps, the system will correctly reflect the transition of ownership on the Hire Statement, with the original owner covering the period up to the vessel's redelivery and the new owner covering the period from the new delivery onward &mdash; but as two separate SoAs , not one continuous statement. &zwnj;\"},{\"id\":\"2365456414\",\"title\":\"How to make the Laycan period in the Cargo Booking to match the Laycan period in the cargo?\",\"text\":\"The \\\"Period Basis\\\" field determines how the booking-level laycan will update the laycan(s) of cargoes within the booking. If the Period Basis for the booking is set to \\\"Default\\\", the booking laycan will be copied as-is to the individual liftings in the booking. [image] If the Period Basis is unset, the total duration for the booking laycan is calculated to distribute the start of the laycan across the cargoes, and the individual laycans are recalculated based on that start date and the system default laycan duration.\"},{\"id\":\"914489390\",\"title\":\"How to troubleshoot error - I/C Company is missing\",\"text\":\"The Intercompany check box in a Cargo contract enables Contract / Invoice Mirroring functionality. If this is unchecked, the mirrored contracts will not get created. You may encounter a situation whereby interfaced Cargo contracts: Do not have the intercompany checkbox checked. And when trying to save the cargo after checking the Intercompany checkbox throws an error - \\\"I/C Company is missing.\\\" [image] Since Cargo Mirroring is a process for cargo relet with an internal owner , the cause for this is due the selected company not being an internal company. To resolve this, you will need to ensure that for both Counterparty and Company entered in Cargo contract, the Internal checkbox should be enabled in the Address book record.\"},{\"id\":\"1831436325\",\"title\":\"If the delete button disappears from a Cargo, what steps should I take to make it return?\",\"text\":\"If the Delete icon does not appear on a user's screen (Image I) but it can be seen when impersonating the account from Veslink (Image II), the specific user that is facing this issue must logout > clear the cookies and cache > then log back in. Image I: [image] Image II: [image] note Note: The field may take some time to re-appear. Note: The field may take some time to re-appear.\"},{\"id\":\"1717436749\",\"title\":\"Is it possible to create multiple Port/Berth combinations in the Cargo COA itinerary?\",\"text\":\"It is not possible to create multiple Port/Berth combinations in the Cargo COA itinerary. Instead, we recommend that users create logical berths for the same port to get different L/D rates. These berths do not need to be physical but logical in order to distinguish the name. In reality, we are using the same port and birth combination while overriding the system's requirement for a unique port and birth combination. For example: users can create berths along the lines of (for example) QUAY1, QUAY2. Users would treat these as the \\\"same\\\" berth, but logging them in the system as unique so that users can create multiple PORT+QUAYX line items in the Cargo COA contract.\"},{\"id\":\"656801966\",\"title\":\"Is it possible to uncancel a Cargo?\",\"text\":\"In IMOS, a user could accidentally set the status of a cargo card to cancelled . This permanently locks the card and prevents any status update. If a user intends to fix the cargo, they will need to either copy the existing cargo or create a new cargo. &zwnj;\"},{\"id\":\"642711747\",\"title\":\"Is it possible to uncancel a Fixture?\",\"text\":\"Yes - In IMOS On-Prem and IMOS, Fixtures can be canceled if any details are incorrect or missing. Canceling a Fixture will lock the record in place and prevent any modifications (including deletion) from being made to the Fixture and linked items. However, users will be able to Uncancel a fixture to make changes if the user has the 'Uncancel a Voyage Fixture' permission enabled. [image]\"}]"